Added long-term retention for change and job history. Records older than a configurable warm window are moved into separate retained-history tables, so the warm tables stay bounded without history being deleted at the cutoff. Retention is disabled by default, and while it is disabled every read and write behaves exactly as it did before.
Added the `changelog_archive` database connection alias, defaulting to the same physical database as `default` so retention needs no separate provisioning to enable. Repoint it with the `NAUTOBOT_CHANGELOG_ARCHIVE_DB_*` environment variables. `ChangelogArchiveRouter` pins the retained-history tables to that alias.
Added `ArchivedObjectChange`, `ArchivedJobResult`, `ArchivedJobLogEntry`, and `ArchivedJobConsoleEntry`, and the `CHANGELOG_ARCHIVE_PERIOD` setting dividing retained history into periods of a year, a quarter or a month, with one table per period per record type. A record's own timestamp decides which period it is written to. The default, `unbounded`, is one period holding everything, and it is the period an installation keeps beside its calendar ones after adopting them, with nothing to migrate. A period is reducible where rows are not: `DROP TABLE` on a period returns its disk at once.
Added the `Changelog Rotation` system job, which moves change and job history older than the warm window into retained storage. It is idempotent: a second run writes the same records again without duplicating them, because each record has the same primary key it had in warm storage.
Added `RetentionRule`, a filter-driven model controlling what changelog truncation deletes, with UI and REST API CRUD, and the `Changelog Truncation` system job, which deletes warm change and job history selected by those rules in bounded increments, independently of whether rotation is configured.
Added the `Changelog Archive Integrity Check` system job, which stands in for the referential integrity the retained-history tables do not enforce. It reports retained records whose referent no longer exists, and deletes them only when asked; it also reports records present in both warm storage and retention, which an interrupted rotation leaves behind.
Added the `nautobot-server check_changelog_archive_schema` command and a matching startup check, which report retained-history tables that have fallen behind the models they mirror.
Added the `extras.view_archivesegment` permission as the single gate on reading retained history, excluded from `EXEMPT_VIEW_PERMISSIONS` handling so a global exemption cannot open it by accident. The retained-history models declare no permissions of their own, so one grant covers every record type and every period.
Added **Archived Change Log** and **Archived Job Results** list and detail views under Extensibility > Logging, read-only and gated on the cold-storage permission. An object's Change Log tab gained a **View archived history** button opening the archived list filtered to that object, which is the route from an object to its older changes.
Added filtering on the archived lists, declared against the retained-history models, including search, time ranges, `changed_object_type` against the identifier column, and the advanced filter with its lookup expressions. Filters that traverse a relation a retained record stores as an identifier column have no counterpart: `user` on change records, and `job_model`, `scheduled_job`, `canceled_by`, `user`, `cancel_type` and `has_job_console_entries` on job results.
Added object links to retained change records. The Object column links to the object a record describes when that object still exists, resolved from the identifier the record stores in one lookup per object type on the page. Records whose object has since been deleted show the recorded `object_repr` as plain text.
Added the read-only `/api/extras/archived-object-changes/` and `/api/extras/archived-job-results/` endpoints, Both return `403` without the cold-storage permission.
Added a declared field contract between each retention mirror and the model it mirrors, asserting that every field is present on both, that fields not excepted are identical, and that the two kinds of permitted exception hold: a foreign key demoted to an identifier column, and a timestamp the mirror must not fill in for itself.
Added the custom field scope filter construct to retention rules: a rule's `scope_filter` is built with the same filter form, so it selects exactly what that filter selects in the change log. `ScopedFilterMixin` and `ScopedFilterViewMixin` are shared by both, in place of the two having separate implementations.
Added validation refusing a retention rule for a model changelog retention does not cover. It would otherwise save cleanly and then never behave, sitting in the list looking enabled while truncation never walked its model.
Added the `without_delete_change_logging()` context manager, which detaches the change log's delete receiver for a bulk deletion and guarantees it is reattached. The changelog cleanup, rotation, and truncation jobs use it in place of a hand-rolled try/finally each.
Added the `nautobot-server create_changelog_retention_demo_data` command, which fabricates backdated change and job history, rotates it, and creates retention rules and two users differing only in the cold-storage permission, so long-term retention can be exercised on an instance that has no history old enough to rotate.
Added the `CHANGELOG_ARCHIVE_ENABLED`, `CHANGELOG_WARM_WINDOW_DAYS`, `CHANGELOG_ROTATION_BATCH_SIZE`, and `CHANGELOG_TRUNCATION_BATCH_SIZE` configuration settings. The first is a deployment setting requiring a restart, because turning retention on commits an installation to a second connection, four retained tables, and two scheduled jobs.
Added the `CHANGELOG_LEGACY_OBJECT_DATA` setting, controlling whether each change record stores the pre-1.3 `object_data` snapshot alongside `object_data_v2`. It defaults to `True`; turning it off halves what each new change record writes.
Added the **Archive Periods** page and `ArchiveSegment` records saying which periods of retained history exist, what each covers, how many records it holds and the newest record in it. Rotation writes a period's row the first time it writes to the period. Read-only: a period is created by rotation, and a row deleted here would leave the table it describes in place and unreachable.
Added `?archive_period=` to the archived lists and their REST endpoints, which chooses the period to read; the newest is served without it. A period selector appears on each archived list's filter form, and filters apply within the chosen period. A period that cannot be read returns nothing and says so, never another period's records.
